1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,729
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,909 --> 00:00:03,823
Here's one of the most unfair truths

3
00:00:03,823 --> 00:00:04,301
in money.

4
00:00:04,481 --> 00:00:06,014
It's expensive to be poor.

5
00:00:06,194 --> 00:00:07,134
Not just hard.

6
00:00:07,314 --> 00:00:08,254
Expensive.

7
00:00:08,434 --> 00:00:10,465
People with the least money often pay

8
00:00:10,465 --> 00:00:12,057
the most for the same things.

9
00:00:12,237 --> 00:00:14,159
More fees, higher interest,

10
00:00:14,159 --> 00:00:16,579
higher prices, and even more time.

11
00:00:16,759 --> 00:00:19,783
Today, twenty hidden costs of being poor,

12
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:21,774
and how to escape each one.

13
00:00:22,124 --> 00:00:24,428
Being poor comes with a hidden price

14
00:00:24,428 --> 00:00:24,748
list.

15
00:00:24,928 --> 00:00:27,793
Fees, interest and higher prices that people

16
00:00:27,793 --> 00:00:29,746
with savings simply never pay.

17
00:00:30,097 --> 00:00:31,490
This isn't about blame.

18
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:33,908
Most of these costs aren't caused by

19
00:00:33,908 --> 00:00:34,653
bad choices.

20
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:37,451
They're built into a system that charges

21
00:00:37,451 --> 00:00:39,610
more to people with less cushion,

22
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:42,032
lower credit scores or fewer options.

23
00:00:42,212 --> 00:00:44,663
But knowing exactly where those costs are

24
00:00:44,663 --> 00:00:45,799
hiding is powerful.

25
00:00:45,979 --> 00:00:48,285
Many of them can be avoided or

26
00:00:48,285 --> 00:00:51,437
reduced, sometimes with one phone call or

27
00:00:51,437 --> 00:00:52,666
one new account.

28
00:00:53,017 --> 00:00:55,385
We'll look at it from three angles.

29
00:00:55,565 --> 00:00:56,771
First, the numbers:

30
00:00:56,771 --> 00:00:59,310
the eight costs of banking and borrowing

31
00:00:59,310 --> 00:01:00,453
without a cushion.

32
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:03,549
Second, the psychology and time costs:

33
00:01:03,549 --> 00:01:06,235
how scarcity drains not just money,

34
00:01:06,235 --> 00:01:08,307
but time and mental energy.

35
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:10,109
And third, the playbook:

36
00:01:10,109 --> 00:01:11,462
the last five costs,

37
00:01:11,462 --> 00:01:13,152
and a step by step way to

38
00:01:13,152 --> 00:01:14,977
escape the poverty premium.

39
00:01:15,327 --> 00:01:17,087
Some of these costs add up to

40
00:01:17,087 --> 00:01:18,786
thousands of dollars a year.

41
00:01:18,966 --> 00:01:20,487
Let's start with the numbers.

42
00:01:20,838 --> 00:01:22,995
Every number here comes from a named

43
00:01:22,995 --> 00:01:23,415
source.

44
00:01:23,595 --> 00:01:26,411
Banking data comes from the FDIC's National

45
00:01:26,411 --> 00:01:29,423
Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households.

46
00:01:29,603 --> 00:01:31,790
Payday and title loan data comes from

47
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:34,212
the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.

48
00:01:34,392 --> 00:01:36,635
Rules on credit union small loans come

49
00:01:36,635 --> 00:01:39,350
from the National Credit Union Administration.

50
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:42,363
Medical debt data comes from the CFPB.

51
00:01:42,543 --> 00:01:45,344
Fees vary widely by company and state,

52
00:01:45,344 --> 00:01:47,483
so treat examples as typical,

53
00:01:47,483 --> 00:01:48,220
not exact.

54
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,782
This is education,

55
00:01:49,782 --> 00:01:51,395
not financial advice.

56
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:53,861
The idea that the poor pay more

57
00:01:53,861 --> 00:01:54,544
isn't new.

58
00:01:54,724 --> 00:01:57,132
In the late eighteen hundreds and early

59
00:01:57,132 --> 00:02:00,035
nineteen hundreds, many coal mining and logging

60
00:02:00,035 --> 00:02:02,258
towns in America ran company stores.

61
00:02:02,438 --> 00:02:04,755
Workers were sometimes paid in scrip,

62
00:02:04,755 --> 00:02:06,947
a currency that could only be spent

63
00:02:06,947 --> 00:02:08,638
at the company's own store.

64
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:11,377
Prices there were often higher than elsewhere,

65
00:02:11,377 --> 00:02:13,324
and workers could fall into debt to

66
00:02:13,324 --> 00:02:14,159
their employer.

67
00:02:14,509 --> 00:02:16,034
The song Sixteen Tons,

68
00:02:16,034 --> 00:02:18,670
made famous by Tennessee Ernie Ford in

69
00:02:18,670 --> 00:02:20,126
the nineteen fifties,

70
00:02:20,126 --> 00:02:22,553
captured the feeling with one line:

71
00:02:22,553 --> 00:02:24,495
I owe my soul to the company

72
00:02:24,495 --> 00:02:24,911
store.

73
00:02:25,091 --> 00:02:27,379
Workers earned, spent at the store,

74
00:02:27,379 --> 00:02:28,360
owed the store,

75
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,060
and worked to pay it back.

76
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:32,179
The cycle was hard to escape.

77
00:02:32,529 --> 00:02:34,605
Today's version is less obvious,

78
00:02:34,605 --> 00:02:36,488
but the structure is similar.

79
00:02:36,668 --> 00:02:38,634
When you have no cushion and few

80
00:02:38,634 --> 00:02:40,907
options, you often end up paying more

81
00:02:40,907 --> 00:02:43,242
to the businesses that serve people in

82
00:02:43,242 --> 00:02:44,655
exactly that situation.

83
00:02:44,835 --> 00:02:47,598
Fees, high interest and higher prices.

84
00:02:47,778 --> 00:02:50,402
Understanding that structure is the first step

85
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:51,714
to stepping outside it.

86
00:02:52,065 --> 00:02:52,842
Angle one.

87
00:02:53,022 --> 00:02:55,208
Eight costs of banking and borrowing when

88
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:56,540
you don't have a cushion.

89
00:02:56,891 --> 00:02:58,075
Cost number one.

90
00:02:58,255 --> 00:02:59,567
Overdraft fees.

91
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:01,618
When your balance is low,

92
00:03:01,618 --> 00:03:04,204
a single small purchase can trigger an

93
00:03:04,204 --> 00:03:07,742
overdraft fee, historically often around thirty five

94
00:03:07,742 --> 00:03:09,239
dollars at many banks.

95
00:03:09,419 --> 00:03:11,562
Buy a six dollar sandwich with eight

96
00:03:11,562 --> 00:03:13,646
dollars too little in your account,

97
00:03:13,646 --> 00:03:16,205
and the sandwich can effectively cost forty

98
00:03:16,205 --> 00:03:16,919
one dollars.

99
00:03:17,099 --> 00:03:19,668
Several small purchases on the same day

100
00:03:19,668 --> 00:03:21,116
can mean several fees.

101
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:24,298
People with healthy balances rarely pay these

102
00:03:24,298 --> 00:03:24,613
fees.

103
00:03:24,793 --> 00:03:27,355
People living paycheck to paycheck can pay

104
00:03:27,355 --> 00:03:28,636
them again and again.

105
00:03:28,816 --> 00:03:31,278
The CFPB has found that a relatively

106
00:03:31,278 --> 00:03:32,988
small share of customers,

107
00:03:32,988 --> 00:03:35,176
usually those with low balances,

108
00:03:35,176 --> 00:03:37,570
pay the majority of overdraft fees.

109
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,272
Many banks have reduced or eliminated overdraft

110
00:03:41,272 --> 00:03:42,769
fees in recent years,

111
00:03:42,769 --> 00:03:44,410
which is real progress.

112
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:46,784
Try this: check whether your bank still

113
00:03:46,784 --> 00:03:47,515
charges them.

114
00:03:47,695 --> 00:03:48,449
If it does,

115
00:03:48,449 --> 00:03:51,738
turn off overdraft coverage for debit purchases,

116
00:03:51,738 --> 00:03:54,067
or move to an account that doesn't

117
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:56,054
charge overdraft fees at all.

118
00:03:56,405 --> 00:03:57,624
Cost number two.

119
00:03:57,804 --> 00:03:59,708
Paying to cash your own paycheck.

120
00:04:00,058 --> 00:04:01,335
According to the FDIC,

121
00:04:01,335 --> 00:04:03,889
millions of American households don't have a

122
00:04:03,889 --> 00:04:04,643
bank account.

123
00:04:04,823 --> 00:04:06,298
They're called unbanked.

124
00:04:06,478 --> 00:04:09,018
In the FDIC's twenty twenty three survey,

125
00:04:09,018 --> 00:04:11,930
about four percent of households were unbanked,

126
00:04:11,930 --> 00:04:14,593
roughly five and a half million households.

127
00:04:14,773 --> 00:04:17,066
Many of them use check cashing stores

128
00:04:17,066 --> 00:04:19,174
to turn their paychecks into cash.

129
00:04:19,524 --> 00:04:21,862
Check cashing stores charge a fee,

130
00:04:21,862 --> 00:04:24,063
often a percentage of the check.

131
00:04:24,243 --> 00:04:26,552
Pay a few percent on every paycheck,

132
00:04:26,552 --> 00:04:28,219
and over a year it can add

133
00:04:28,219 --> 00:04:29,886
up to hundreds of dollars,

134
00:04:29,886 --> 00:04:32,451
just to access money you already earned.

135
00:04:32,631 --> 00:04:34,486
Add money orders to pay bills,

136
00:04:34,486 --> 00:04:36,711
and the cost of handling money grows

137
00:04:36,711 --> 00:04:37,206
further.

138
00:04:37,556 --> 00:04:39,541
Why do people stay unbanked?

139
00:04:39,721 --> 00:04:42,748
The FDIC survey found common reasons included

140
00:04:42,748 --> 00:04:45,372
not having enough money to meet minimum

141
00:04:45,372 --> 00:04:48,131
balance requirements, not trusting banks,

142
00:04:48,131 --> 00:04:50,216
and high or unpredictable fees.

143
00:04:50,396 --> 00:04:51,871
Those are fair concerns,

144
00:04:51,871 --> 00:04:53,345
and there are solutions.

145
00:04:53,696 --> 00:04:55,526
Try this: look for a Bank On

146
00:04:55,526 --> 00:04:56,703
certified account.

147
00:04:56,883 --> 00:04:59,641
These are low cost accounts offered by

148
00:04:59,641 --> 00:05:01,746
many banks and credit unions,

149
00:05:01,746 --> 00:05:03,705
meeting national standards,

150
00:05:03,705 --> 00:05:06,246
usually with low or no monthly fees

151
00:05:06,246 --> 00:05:07,842
and no overdraft fees.

152
00:05:08,022 --> 00:05:09,529
You can find them at the Bank

153
00:05:09,529 --> 00:05:10,101
On website.

154
00:05:10,451 --> 00:05:11,839
According to the FDIC,

155
00:05:11,839 --> 00:05:14,489
about four percent of American households,

156
00:05:14,489 --> 00:05:16,508
roughly five and a half million,

157
00:05:16,508 --> 00:05:18,779
had no bank account in twenty twenty

158
00:05:18,779 --> 00:05:19,158
three.

159
00:05:19,508 --> 00:05:20,727
Cost number three.

160
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:22,044
Payday loans.

161
00:05:22,395 --> 00:05:24,418
A payday loan is a small,

162
00:05:24,418 --> 00:05:25,714
short term loan,

163
00:05:25,714 --> 00:05:28,304
usually due on your next payday.

164
00:05:28,484 --> 00:05:29,914
According to the CFPB,

165
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:32,385
a typical two week payday loan charges

166
00:05:32,385 --> 00:05:34,661
a fee of around fifteen dollars for

167
00:05:34,661 --> 00:05:36,936
every one hundred dollars borrowed.

168
00:05:37,116 --> 00:05:38,138
That sounds small.

169
00:05:38,318 --> 00:05:40,396
But as an annual percentage rate,

170
00:05:40,396 --> 00:05:42,474
it's almost four hundred percent.

171
00:05:42,825 --> 00:05:44,740
The real trap is the rollover.

172
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,910
Many borrowers can't repay the full amount

173
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:48,836
in two weeks,

174
00:05:48,836 --> 00:05:50,971
so they pay the fee and borrow

175
00:05:50,971 --> 00:05:51,399
again.

176
00:05:51,579 --> 00:05:54,012
The CFPB has found that most payday

177
00:05:54,012 --> 00:05:56,655
loan fees come from borrowers stuck in

178
00:05:56,655 --> 00:05:58,324
long sequences of loans.

179
00:05:58,504 --> 00:06:00,784
A four hundred dollar loan can turn

180
00:06:00,784 --> 00:06:03,195
into hundreds of dollars in fees over

181
00:06:03,195 --> 00:06:04,042
a few months.

182
00:06:04,392 --> 00:06:06,869
Payday lending rules vary a lot by

183
00:06:06,869 --> 00:06:07,306
state.

184
00:06:07,486 --> 00:06:10,298
Some states ban high cost payday loans,

185
00:06:10,298 --> 00:06:11,596
others allow them.

186
00:06:11,776 --> 00:06:13,828
But everywhere, they're one of the most

187
00:06:13,828 --> 00:06:15,723
expensive forms of credit available.

188
00:06:16,073 --> 00:06:18,112
Try this: if you're a member of

189
00:06:18,112 --> 00:06:19,625
a federal credit union,

190
00:06:19,625 --> 00:06:21,994
ask about a payday alternative loan.

191
00:06:22,174 --> 00:06:24,574
These small loans are regulated by the

192
00:06:24,574 --> 00:06:26,911
National Credit Union Administration,

193
00:06:26,911 --> 00:06:29,563
with interest rates capped at twenty eight

194
00:06:29,563 --> 00:06:30,068
percent.

195
00:06:30,248 --> 00:06:31,293
Still not cheap,

196
00:06:31,293 --> 00:06:33,383
but a fraction of a payday loan.

197
00:06:33,733 --> 00:06:36,576
A typical payday loan costs fifteen dollars

198
00:06:36,576 --> 00:06:39,154
per one hundred borrowed for two weeks.

199
00:06:39,334 --> 00:06:41,130
That's close to four hundred percent a

200
00:06:41,130 --> 00:06:41,366
year.

201
00:06:41,546 --> 00:06:44,851
A federal credit union payday alternative loan

202
00:06:44,851 --> 00:06:47,293
is capped at twenty eight percent.

203
00:06:47,643 --> 00:06:48,792
Cost number four.

204
00:06:48,972 --> 00:06:49,970
Rent to own.

205
00:06:50,321 --> 00:06:52,371
Rent to own stores let you take

206
00:06:52,371 --> 00:06:55,611
home furniture, appliances or electronics with no

207
00:06:55,611 --> 00:06:58,587
credit check, for weekly or monthly payments.

208
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:00,541
If you keep paying long enough,

209
00:07:00,541 --> 00:07:01,170
you own it.

210
00:07:01,350 --> 00:07:02,697
It sounds accessible.

211
00:07:02,877 --> 00:07:04,819
But the total cost can be two

212
00:07:04,819 --> 00:07:07,363
or even three times the regular retail

213
00:07:07,363 --> 00:07:07,765
price.

214
00:07:08,115 --> 00:07:10,529
Imagine a TV that costs five hundred

215
00:07:10,529 --> 00:07:12,341
dollars at a regular store.

216
00:07:12,521 --> 00:07:13,813
On a rent to own plan,

217
00:07:13,813 --> 00:07:15,810
the total payments to own it could

218
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:17,513
be over one thousand dollars.

219
00:07:17,693 --> 00:07:19,112
And if you miss payments,

220
00:07:19,112 --> 00:07:20,644
the store can take it back,

221
00:07:20,644 --> 00:07:22,460
and you keep none of what you've

222
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:22,743
paid.

223
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:25,382
Rent to own contracts are also often

224
00:07:25,382 --> 00:07:28,116
structured so that the weekly payment looks

225
00:07:28,116 --> 00:07:28,434
tiny.

226
00:07:28,614 --> 00:07:30,816
Twenty or thirty dollars a week feels

227
00:07:30,816 --> 00:07:31,470
manageable.

228
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:33,630
But multiply by the number of weeks

229
00:07:33,630 --> 00:07:34,534
in the contract,

230
00:07:34,534 --> 00:07:36,570
and the total tells a very different

231
00:07:36,570 --> 00:07:36,910
story.

232
00:07:37,090 --> 00:07:39,559
It's the same partitioned price trick we

233
00:07:39,559 --> 00:07:40,608
see with buy now,

234
00:07:40,608 --> 00:07:41,966
pay later, just older.

235
00:07:42,316 --> 00:07:45,018
Try this: for things you truly need,

236
00:07:45,018 --> 00:07:46,669
look for used options,

237
00:07:46,669 --> 00:07:48,845
community buy nothing groups,

238
00:07:48,845 --> 00:07:50,872
or save for a few weeks and

239
00:07:50,872 --> 00:07:51,847
buy outright.

240
00:07:52,027 --> 00:07:53,501
For a temporary need,

241
00:07:53,501 --> 00:07:55,116
renting can make sense.

242
00:07:55,296 --> 00:07:57,153
For owning, it's one of the most

243
00:07:57,153 --> 00:07:58,604
expensive paths there is.

244
00:07:58,955 --> 00:08:00,290
Cost number five.

245
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:02,281
The low credit score tax.

246
00:08:02,631 --> 00:08:04,959
A lower credit score raises the price

247
00:08:04,959 --> 00:08:06,973
of almost everything you borrow.

248
00:08:07,153 --> 00:08:09,003
Car loans, credit cards,

249
00:08:09,003 --> 00:08:12,319
personal loans and mortgages all cost more.

250
00:08:12,499 --> 00:08:14,928
The difference between a high score and

251
00:08:14,928 --> 00:08:16,984
a low score can be the difference

252
00:08:16,984 --> 00:08:19,101
between a car loan at around seven

253
00:08:19,101 --> 00:08:21,406
percent and one at fifteen percent or

254
00:08:21,406 --> 00:08:21,718
more.

255
00:08:22,068 --> 00:08:24,541
Low credit also affects things that aren't

256
00:08:24,541 --> 00:08:25,307
loans at all.

257
00:08:25,487 --> 00:08:28,169
Some landlords check credit before renting.

258
00:08:28,349 --> 00:08:31,619
Some employers review credit information for certain

259
00:08:31,619 --> 00:08:33,318
jobs, where allowed by law.

260
00:08:33,498 --> 00:08:34,855
And in many states,

261
00:08:34,855 --> 00:08:37,213
insurers use credit based scores.

262
00:08:37,393 --> 00:08:39,978
A low score quietly raises the cost

263
00:08:39,978 --> 00:08:42,710
of living in many directions at once.

264
00:08:43,061 --> 00:08:45,332
On a twenty thousand dollar car loan

265
00:08:45,332 --> 00:08:46,342
over five years,

266
00:08:46,342 --> 00:08:49,055
going from seven percent to fifteen percent

267
00:08:49,055 --> 00:08:52,084
adds roughly four thousand eight hundred dollars

268
00:08:52,084 --> 00:08:53,219
in extra interest.

269
00:08:53,399 --> 00:08:54,444
Same car.

270
00:08:54,624 --> 00:08:55,692
Same price.

271
00:08:55,872 --> 00:08:58,379
Thousands more, because of a number on

272
00:08:58,379 --> 00:08:58,972
a report.

273
00:08:59,323 --> 00:09:01,970
Try this: get your free credit reports

274
00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:04,408
at annual credit report dot com and

275
00:09:04,408 --> 00:09:05,731
dispute any errors.

276
00:09:05,911 --> 00:09:07,307
Pay every bill on time,

277
00:09:07,307 --> 00:09:09,127
even if it's just the minimum.

278
00:09:09,307 --> 00:09:11,246
And keep card balances low.

279
00:09:11,426 --> 00:09:14,536
Scores can improve faster than many people

280
00:09:14,536 --> 00:09:16,535
think, often within months.

281
00:09:16,885 --> 00:09:18,429
Cost number six.

282
00:09:18,609 --> 00:09:20,199
Security deposits.

283
00:09:20,550 --> 00:09:21,965
Without good credit,

284
00:09:21,965 --> 00:09:24,230
many companies ask for deposits.

285
00:09:24,410 --> 00:09:27,405
A bigger security deposit on an apartment.

286
00:09:27,585 --> 00:09:29,587
Deposits for electricity,

287
00:09:29,587 --> 00:09:32,148
gas, internet and phone service.

288
00:09:32,328 --> 00:09:34,785
Some landlords ask for extra months up

289
00:09:34,785 --> 00:09:35,173
front.

290
00:09:35,353 --> 00:09:36,713
That money is locked away,

291
00:09:36,713 --> 00:09:38,545
often when you can least afford it.

292
00:09:38,896 --> 00:09:41,242
These deposits can add up to hundreds

293
00:09:41,242 --> 00:09:43,081
or even thousands of dollars,

294
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:44,920
making it harder to move to a

295
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:46,442
better or cheaper place.

296
00:09:46,622 --> 00:09:49,097
Someone with good credit simply signs and

297
00:09:49,097 --> 00:09:49,641
moves in.

298
00:09:49,991 --> 00:09:53,134
Try this: ask utilities whether they'll waive

299
00:09:53,134 --> 00:09:55,510
the deposit if you set up autopay,

300
00:09:55,510 --> 00:09:57,466
or after a period of on time

301
00:09:57,466 --> 00:09:58,094
payments.

302
00:09:58,274 --> 00:10:00,389
And ask landlords if they accept a

303
00:10:00,389 --> 00:10:02,628
letter showing on time rent history.

304
00:10:02,808 --> 00:10:05,525
Many have flexibility they don't advertise.

305
00:10:05,875 --> 00:10:07,128
Cost number seven.

306
00:10:07,308 --> 00:10:09,247
Prepaid card fees.

307
00:10:09,598 --> 00:10:12,329
Prepaid debit cards are popular with people

308
00:10:12,329 --> 00:10:14,172
who don't have bank accounts.

309
00:10:14,352 --> 00:10:15,757
But many have fees.

310
00:10:15,937 --> 00:10:17,449
Fees to buy the card,

311
00:10:17,449 --> 00:10:19,321
monthly fees, reload fees,

312
00:10:19,321 --> 00:10:21,841
ATM fees and even fees for checking

313
00:10:21,841 --> 00:10:23,785
your balance on some cards.

314
00:10:23,965 --> 00:10:25,370
Each one is small.

315
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:27,956
Together, they can easily cost more than

316
00:10:27,956 --> 00:10:29,219
a basic bank account.

317
00:10:29,569 --> 00:10:31,182
Since twenty nineteen,

318
00:10:31,182 --> 00:10:34,262
the CFPB's prepaid rule requires clear fee

319
00:10:34,262 --> 00:10:36,389
disclosures on prepaid cards,

320
00:10:36,389 --> 00:10:38,590
which makes comparison easier.

321
00:10:38,770 --> 00:10:40,944
Look for the short form disclosure on

322
00:10:40,944 --> 00:10:41,649
the package.

323
00:10:41,999 --> 00:10:44,860
Try this: compare your yearly prepaid card

324
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:47,380
fees with a Bank On certified account

325
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:49,696
or a free online checking account.

326
00:10:49,876 --> 00:10:50,922
For many people,

327
00:10:50,922 --> 00:10:53,080
switching saves money right away.

328
00:10:53,431 --> 00:10:54,464
Cost number eight.

329
00:10:54,644 --> 00:10:55,909
Car title loans.

330
00:10:56,260 --> 00:10:58,404
A car title loan lets you borrow

331
00:10:58,404 --> 00:10:59,543
against your car,

332
00:10:59,543 --> 00:11:01,554
using the title as collateral.

333
00:11:01,734 --> 00:11:04,092
Interest rates are often extremely high,

334
00:11:04,092 --> 00:11:05,507
and the loans are short.

335
00:11:05,687 --> 00:11:07,631
The CFPB found that about one in

336
00:11:07,631 --> 00:11:10,426
five single payment title loan sequences ended

337
00:11:10,426 --> 00:11:12,978
with the borrower's car being repossessed.

338
00:11:13,328 --> 00:11:15,421
Losing the car can mean losing the

339
00:11:15,421 --> 00:11:16,837
ability to get to work,

340
00:11:16,837 --> 00:11:18,622
which makes everything worse.

341
00:11:18,802 --> 00:11:20,601
A loan meant to solve a short

342
00:11:20,601 --> 00:11:22,896
term problem can create a much bigger

343
00:11:22,896 --> 00:11:23,144
one.

344
00:11:23,494 --> 00:11:25,600
Try this: before a title loan,

345
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:27,916
call your creditors and ask for a

346
00:11:27,916 --> 00:11:31,286
payment plan, check local assistance programs by

347
00:11:31,286 --> 00:11:32,690
dialing two one one,

348
00:11:32,690 --> 00:11:35,077
and ask a credit union about small

349
00:11:35,077 --> 00:11:35,498
loans.

350
00:11:35,678 --> 00:11:38,416
Almost any alternative is safer than risking

351
00:11:38,416 --> 00:11:38,976
your car.

352
00:11:39,326 --> 00:11:42,124
That's eight costs of banking and borrowing.

353
00:11:42,304 --> 00:11:44,326
Now the hidden costs that don't show

354
00:11:44,326 --> 00:11:45,450
up on any statement.

355
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:46,659
Angle two.

356
00:11:46,839 --> 00:11:48,527
Seven costs of time,

357
00:11:48,527 --> 00:11:50,554
energy and buying small.

358
00:11:50,904 --> 00:11:52,099
Cost number nine.

359
00:11:52,279 --> 00:11:54,149
Buying in small quantities.

360
00:11:54,499 --> 00:11:56,861
Buying in bulk is usually cheaper per

361
00:11:56,861 --> 00:11:57,181
unit.

362
00:11:57,361 --> 00:11:59,499
The big pack of toilet paper,

363
00:11:59,499 --> 00:12:01,121
the large bag of rice,

364
00:12:01,121 --> 00:12:03,038
the family size detergent.

365
00:12:03,218 --> 00:12:06,100
But bulk buying requires extra cash today,

366
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:08,364
storage space, and often a car to

367
00:12:08,364 --> 00:12:10,079
get to a warehouse store.

368
00:12:10,259 --> 00:12:12,433
Without those, people buy small,

369
00:12:12,433 --> 00:12:13,928
and pay more per unit.

370
00:12:14,278 --> 00:12:17,085
Small differences per item add up across

371
00:12:17,085 --> 00:12:19,822
a whole year of groceries and household

372
00:12:19,822 --> 00:12:20,454
supplies.

373
00:12:20,634 --> 00:12:22,975
It's a quiet tax on not having

374
00:12:22,975 --> 00:12:24,535
extra cash or space.

375
00:12:24,885 --> 00:12:26,545
Location matters too.

376
00:12:26,725 --> 00:12:28,893
In some low income neighborhoods,

377
00:12:28,893 --> 00:12:31,586
there are fewer full size grocery stores,

378
00:12:31,586 --> 00:12:34,345
so people shop at convenience stores where

379
00:12:34,345 --> 00:12:36,907
prices are often higher and choices are

380
00:12:36,907 --> 00:12:37,302
fewer.

381
00:12:37,482 --> 00:12:40,303
Researchers call these areas food deserts.

382
00:12:40,483 --> 00:12:42,816
The same groceries can cost more simply

383
00:12:42,816 --> 00:12:44,372
because of where you live.

384
00:12:44,722 --> 00:12:47,165
Try this: check unit prices on shelf

385
00:12:47,165 --> 00:12:49,609
tags, which show the price per ounce

386
00:12:49,609 --> 00:12:50,492
or per count.

387
00:12:50,672 --> 00:12:53,122
Team up with a friend or neighbor

388
00:12:53,122 --> 00:12:54,608
to split bulk packs.

389
00:12:54,788 --> 00:12:56,710
And buy bulk only for things you

390
00:12:56,710 --> 00:12:58,572
use often and that don't spoil.

391
00:12:58,923 --> 00:13:00,188
Cost number ten.

392
00:13:00,368 --> 00:13:01,576
The boots theory.

393
00:13:01,926 --> 00:13:04,545
The author Terry Pratchett described it through

394
00:13:04,545 --> 00:13:06,105
a character named Sam Vimes.

395
00:13:06,285 --> 00:13:08,168
A rich man buys good boots for

396
00:13:08,168 --> 00:13:10,302
fifty dollars that last ten years.

397
00:13:10,482 --> 00:13:12,347
A poor man can only afford ten

398
00:13:12,347 --> 00:13:14,337
dollar boots that last a season.

399
00:13:14,517 --> 00:13:15,534
Over ten years,

400
00:13:15,534 --> 00:13:17,637
the poor man spends far more on

401
00:13:17,637 --> 00:13:19,672
boots, and still has wet feet.

402
00:13:20,022 --> 00:13:22,727
It's fiction, but it describes something real.

403
00:13:22,907 --> 00:13:24,791
Cheaper versions of shoes,

404
00:13:24,791 --> 00:13:27,763
appliances, tools and cars often wear out

405
00:13:27,763 --> 00:13:28,271
faster.

406
00:13:28,451 --> 00:13:30,793
Without the cash to buy quality up

407
00:13:30,793 --> 00:13:33,343
front, people end up buying again and

408
00:13:33,343 --> 00:13:33,756
again.

409
00:13:34,107 --> 00:13:36,568
Try this: for things you use daily,

410
00:13:36,568 --> 00:13:38,818
like shoes, a mattress or tools,

411
00:13:38,818 --> 00:13:40,998
look for quality used versions,

412
00:13:40,998 --> 00:13:43,600
which can offer durability at a lower

413
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:44,022
price.

414
00:13:44,202 --> 00:13:46,002
And save in a sinking fund so

415
00:13:46,002 --> 00:13:48,237
the next replacement can be a better

416
00:13:48,237 --> 00:13:48,486
one.

417
00:13:48,836 --> 00:13:50,066
Cost number eleven.

418
00:13:50,246 --> 00:13:51,349
The time tax.

419
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:52,669
Without a car,

420
00:13:52,669 --> 00:13:54,608
a trip to the grocery store,

421
00:13:54,608 --> 00:13:57,448
a job interview or a doctor's appointment

422
00:13:57,448 --> 00:13:59,734
can take hours by public transit.

423
00:13:59,914 --> 00:14:01,369
Without paid leave,

424
00:14:01,369 --> 00:14:03,513
a sick day means lost wages.

425
00:14:03,693 --> 00:14:05,004
Without a washer,

426
00:14:05,004 --> 00:14:07,779
laundry means hours at a laundromat.

427
00:14:07,959 --> 00:14:09,775
Being poor often costs time,

428
00:14:09,775 --> 00:14:11,850
and time is money you can't earn

429
00:14:11,850 --> 00:14:12,174
back.

430
00:14:12,524 --> 00:14:14,149
There's also the paperwork.

431
00:14:14,329 --> 00:14:16,019
Applying for benefits,

432
00:14:16,019 --> 00:14:18,860
proving eligibility, waiting on hold,

433
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:20,320
renewing documents.

434
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:24,087
Researchers sometimes call this administrative burden.

435
00:14:24,267 --> 00:14:26,336
It falls hardest on the people with

436
00:14:26,336 --> 00:14:28,168
the least time and flexibility.

437
00:14:28,518 --> 00:14:30,337
Try this: batch errands,

438
00:14:30,337 --> 00:14:32,914
use online services when possible,

439
00:14:32,914 --> 00:14:36,250
and ask employers about flexible scheduling.

440
00:14:36,430 --> 00:14:38,663
Small time savings free up energy for

441
00:14:38,663 --> 00:14:41,318
the bigger moves that change your situation.

442
00:14:41,668 --> 00:14:42,875
Cost number twelve.

443
00:14:43,055 --> 00:14:44,413
The bandwidth tax.

444
00:14:44,764 --> 00:14:48,923
Researchers Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir described

445
00:14:48,923 --> 00:14:51,602
how scarcity uses up mental bandwidth.

446
00:14:51,782 --> 00:14:53,399
In one well known study,

447
00:14:53,399 --> 00:14:56,498
people facing money worries performed worse on

448
00:14:56,498 --> 00:14:58,924
cognitive tests, as if they had lost

449
00:14:58,924 --> 00:15:00,002
a night's sleep.

450
00:15:00,182 --> 00:15:02,481
Not because they were less capable,

451
00:15:02,481 --> 00:15:04,977
but because worry was taking up mental

452
00:15:04,977 --> 00:15:05,371
space.

453
00:15:05,722 --> 00:15:08,788
That's important, because managing money with little

454
00:15:08,788 --> 00:15:10,852
cushion requires the most planning,

455
00:15:10,852 --> 00:15:12,386
comparing and remembering.

456
00:15:12,566 --> 00:15:15,746
The situation demands more bandwidth and leaves

457
00:15:15,746 --> 00:15:16,490
less of it.

458
00:15:16,670 --> 00:15:18,664
It's one of the cruelest parts of

459
00:15:18,664 --> 00:15:19,329
being poor.

460
00:15:19,679 --> 00:15:22,107
Try this: reduce the number of decisions

461
00:15:22,107 --> 00:15:23,138
you have to make.

462
00:15:23,318 --> 00:15:25,806
Automate bill payments when you can,

463
00:15:25,806 --> 00:15:27,742
set reminders for due dates,

464
00:15:27,742 --> 00:15:29,193
and use simple rules.

465
00:15:29,373 --> 00:15:31,835
Systems save bandwidth for the decisions that

466
00:15:31,835 --> 00:15:32,218
matter.

467
00:15:32,568 --> 00:15:34,065
Cost number thirteen.

468
00:15:34,245 --> 00:15:35,627
Late fee cascades.

469
00:15:35,977 --> 00:15:37,308
When money is tight,

470
00:15:37,308 --> 00:15:39,506
one late bill can trigger others.

471
00:15:39,686 --> 00:15:41,709
A late rent payment means a late

472
00:15:41,709 --> 00:15:41,962
fee.

473
00:15:42,142 --> 00:15:44,221
Paying that fee makes the phone bill

474
00:15:44,221 --> 00:15:44,510
late.

475
00:15:44,690 --> 00:15:46,763
The phone bill late fee makes the

476
00:15:46,763 --> 00:15:47,894
card payment late.

477
00:15:48,074 --> 00:15:50,048
Each fee makes the next payment harder.

478
00:15:50,228 --> 00:15:51,315
Within a month,

479
00:15:51,315 --> 00:15:53,851
a small shortfall can become a pile

480
00:15:53,851 --> 00:15:54,431
of fees.

481
00:15:54,781 --> 00:15:57,317
Late payments can also be reported to

482
00:15:57,317 --> 00:16:00,197
credit bureaus once they're thirty days or

483
00:16:00,197 --> 00:16:01,156
more past due,

484
00:16:01,156 --> 00:16:02,802
which lowers your score,

485
00:16:02,802 --> 00:16:05,613
which raises your future borrowing costs.

486
00:16:05,793 --> 00:16:07,843
So a single tight month can echo

487
00:16:07,843 --> 00:16:10,471
for years, through higher interest on the

488
00:16:10,471 --> 00:16:12,329
next car loan or credit card.

489
00:16:12,679 --> 00:16:13,894
Late fees on rent,

490
00:16:13,894 --> 00:16:16,732
utilities, credit cards and loans can each

491
00:16:16,732 --> 00:16:19,028
be twenty five to fifty dollars or

492
00:16:19,028 --> 00:16:19,366
more.

493
00:16:19,546 --> 00:16:21,527
Three or four in one month can

494
00:16:21,527 --> 00:16:23,507
cost over one hundred dollars,

495
00:16:23,507 --> 00:16:26,082
often more than the original shortfall.

496
00:16:26,433 --> 00:16:28,418
Try this: when you know a payment

497
00:16:28,418 --> 00:16:29,201
will be late,

498
00:16:29,201 --> 00:16:30,705
call before the due date.

499
00:16:30,885 --> 00:16:34,310
Many landlords, utilities and lenders will waive

500
00:16:34,310 --> 00:16:36,094
a fee or agree to a short

501
00:16:36,094 --> 00:16:37,735
delay if you ask early.

502
00:16:37,915 --> 00:16:40,121
Calling after it's late is much harder.

503
00:16:40,471 --> 00:16:42,003
Cost number fourteen.

504
00:16:42,183 --> 00:16:44,192
Higher insurance costs.

505
00:16:44,542 --> 00:16:45,578
In many states,

506
00:16:45,578 --> 00:16:48,686
insurers use credit based insurance scores to

507
00:16:48,686 --> 00:16:51,380
help set car and home insurance prices.

508
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:54,108
That means people with lower credit can

509
00:16:54,108 --> 00:16:56,134
pay more for the same coverage.

510
00:16:56,314 --> 00:16:58,259
And to keep premiums affordable,

511
00:16:58,259 --> 00:17:00,446
many people choose high deductibles,

512
00:17:00,446 --> 00:17:02,208
which can be hard to pay when

513
00:17:02,208 --> 00:17:03,849
something actually happens.

514
00:17:04,199 --> 00:17:05,522
Then there's being uninsured.

515
00:17:05,702 --> 00:17:07,402
Without health insurance,

516
00:17:07,402 --> 00:17:09,986
a single emergency room visit can cost

517
00:17:09,986 --> 00:17:11,414
thousands of dollars.

518
00:17:11,594 --> 00:17:13,452
Without renter's insurance,

519
00:17:13,452 --> 00:17:15,586
a fire or theft can mean losing

520
00:17:15,586 --> 00:17:16,343
everything.

521
00:17:16,693 --> 00:17:18,863
Try this: shop insurance every year,

522
00:17:18,863 --> 00:17:20,069
ask about discounts,

523
00:17:20,069 --> 00:17:22,420
and check if you qualify for subsidized

524
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:25,495
health coverage through the official marketplace at

525
00:17:25,495 --> 00:17:26,700
health care dot gov,

526
00:17:26,700 --> 00:17:28,268
or Medicaid in your state.

527
00:17:28,448 --> 00:17:31,548
Renter's insurance is often surprisingly cheap.

528
00:17:31,898 --> 00:17:33,302
Cost number fifteen.

529
00:17:33,482 --> 00:17:35,874
Monthly account and ATM fees.

530
00:17:36,224 --> 00:17:38,648
Many accounts waive monthly fees if you

531
00:17:38,648 --> 00:17:40,078
keep a minimum balance.

532
00:17:40,258 --> 00:17:42,598
People with low balances pay those fees,

533
00:17:42,598 --> 00:17:44,705
and people with high balances don't.

534
00:17:44,885 --> 00:17:47,599
And without a nearby in network ATM,

535
00:17:47,599 --> 00:17:51,067
withdrawing cash can cost several dollars each

536
00:17:51,067 --> 00:17:51,444
time.

537
00:17:51,795 --> 00:17:54,261
Try this: look for accounts with no

538
00:17:54,261 --> 00:17:56,797
monthly fees and no minimum balance,

539
00:17:56,797 --> 00:18:00,038
including many online banks and credit unions.

540
00:18:00,218 --> 00:18:01,704
Use in network ATMs,

541
00:18:01,704 --> 00:18:04,007
or get cash back at stores when

542
00:18:04,007 --> 00:18:06,534
buying something you already need.

543
00:18:06,884 --> 00:18:10,149
Some employers also offer pay cards instead

544
00:18:10,149 --> 00:18:11,516
of direct deposit.

545
00:18:11,696 --> 00:18:13,701
By law, they generally have to give

546
00:18:13,701 --> 00:18:15,247
you at least one way to get

547
00:18:15,247 --> 00:18:16,851
your full pay without a fee.

548
00:18:17,031 --> 00:18:19,607
Ask your employer or the card provider

549
00:18:19,607 --> 00:18:20,827
how to avoid fees,

550
00:18:20,827 --> 00:18:23,132
or ask to switch to direct deposit

551
00:18:23,132 --> 00:18:24,624
into your own account.

552
00:18:24,974 --> 00:18:26,065
Quick pause.

553
00:18:26,245 --> 00:18:28,163
If this video might help someone you

554
00:18:28,163 --> 00:18:29,867
know, please share it with them.

555
00:18:30,047 --> 00:18:31,719
And subscribe to the channel if it's

556
00:18:31,719 --> 00:18:32,276
helping you.

557
00:18:32,456 --> 00:18:34,348
It's free, and it helps me keep

558
00:18:34,348 --> 00:18:35,568
making these videos.

559
00:18:35,748 --> 00:18:37,640
Now, the last five costs,

560
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:39,684
and how to escape them all.

561
00:18:40,034 --> 00:18:40,893
Angle three.

562
00:18:41,073 --> 00:18:42,533
The last five costs,

563
00:18:42,533 --> 00:18:44,358
and then the escape plan.

564
00:18:44,709 --> 00:18:46,148
Cost number sixteen.

565
00:18:46,328 --> 00:18:47,942
Fines and fees that grow.

566
00:18:48,292 --> 00:18:50,881
A parking ticket or traffic fine might

567
00:18:50,881 --> 00:18:52,448
be manageable at first.

568
00:18:52,628 --> 00:18:54,628
But if it isn't paid on time,

569
00:18:54,628 --> 00:18:56,420
many places add late fees,

570
00:18:56,420 --> 00:18:59,316
collection fees, and in some cases suspend

571
00:18:59,316 --> 00:19:00,558
driver's licenses.

572
00:19:00,738 --> 00:19:02,729
A one hundred dollar ticket can grow

573
00:19:02,729 --> 00:19:04,334
into several hundred dollars,

574
00:19:04,334 --> 00:19:06,270
and a suspended license can make it

575
00:19:06,270 --> 00:19:07,100
harder to work.

576
00:19:07,450 --> 00:19:10,467
Try this: contact the court or agency

577
00:19:10,467 --> 00:19:10,956
early.

578
00:19:11,136 --> 00:19:12,957
Many offer payment plans,

579
00:19:12,957 --> 00:19:15,142
reduced fines based on income,

580
00:19:15,142 --> 00:19:17,254
or community service options.

581
00:19:17,434 --> 00:19:20,360
Some jurisdictions run amnesty programs.

582
00:19:20,540 --> 00:19:23,199
Ignoring it is the most expensive choice.

583
00:19:23,549 --> 00:19:25,812
Also check whether a fine has turned

584
00:19:25,812 --> 00:19:28,703
into a license suspension without you knowing.

585
00:19:28,883 --> 00:19:31,261
Driving on a suspended license can lead

586
00:19:31,261 --> 00:19:32,784
to much bigger penalties.

587
00:19:32,964 --> 00:19:35,769
Many states have online tools to check

588
00:19:35,769 --> 00:19:37,244
your license status,

589
00:19:37,244 --> 00:19:40,344
and some have programs to restore licenses

590
00:19:40,344 --> 00:19:42,705
suspended only for unpaid fines.

591
00:19:43,055 --> 00:19:44,552
Cost number seventeen.

592
00:19:44,732 --> 00:19:47,333
Buy here, pay here car lots.

593
00:19:47,683 --> 00:19:50,115
Buy here, pay here dealers sell used

594
00:19:50,115 --> 00:19:52,074
cars with in house financing,

595
00:19:52,074 --> 00:19:54,033
often without a credit check.

596
00:19:54,213 --> 00:19:56,024
For people with poor credit,

597
00:19:56,024 --> 00:19:58,288
they may seem like the only option.

598
00:19:58,468 --> 00:20:00,634
But interest rates can be very high,

599
00:20:00,634 --> 00:20:02,860
cars may be priced above their value,

600
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:05,327
and some dealers repossess quickly when a

601
00:20:05,327 --> 00:20:06,410
payment is missed.

602
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:08,820
Try this: before visiting one,

603
00:20:08,820 --> 00:20:10,606
check with a credit union,

604
00:20:10,606 --> 00:20:12,460
even with imperfect credit.

605
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:15,496
Some offer loans designed for rebuilding credit.

606
00:20:15,676 --> 00:20:18,153
And consider a cheaper car bought partly

607
00:20:18,153 --> 00:20:21,621
with savings, with an independent mechanic's inspection.

608
00:20:21,971 --> 00:20:23,863
And read the contract carefully.

609
00:20:24,043 --> 00:20:25,061
Look for the APR,

610
00:20:25,061 --> 00:20:26,377
the total of payments,

611
00:20:26,377 --> 00:20:28,292
and what happens if you're late.

612
00:20:28,472 --> 00:20:31,361
Some contracts allow the dealer to install

613
00:20:31,361 --> 00:20:33,631
devices that disable the car if a

614
00:20:33,631 --> 00:20:34,869
payment is missed.

615
00:20:35,049 --> 00:20:37,430
Know exactly what you're agreeing to before

616
00:20:37,430 --> 00:20:37,929
you sign.

617
00:20:38,279 --> 00:20:39,846
Cost number eighteen.

618
00:20:40,026 --> 00:20:40,885
Medical debt.

619
00:20:41,235 --> 00:20:43,115
Medical debt is one of the most

620
00:20:43,115 --> 00:20:45,298
common kinds of debt in collections.

621
00:20:45,478 --> 00:20:48,194
The CFPB has estimated that medical bills

622
00:20:48,194 --> 00:20:51,175
on credit reports totaled around eighty eight

623
00:20:51,175 --> 00:20:52,235
billion dollars.

624
00:20:52,415 --> 00:20:54,957
People with less savings and less insurance

625
00:20:54,957 --> 00:20:55,903
are hit hardest,

626
00:20:55,903 --> 00:20:58,150
and medical debt can then damage their

627
00:20:58,150 --> 00:21:00,751
credit, raising the cost of everything else.

628
00:21:01,101 --> 00:21:02,680
There's been some progress.

629
00:21:02,860 --> 00:21:05,778
The major credit bureaus stopped reporting paid

630
00:21:05,778 --> 00:21:09,193
medical collections, and medical collections under five

631
00:21:09,193 --> 00:21:11,242
hundred dollars, in recent years.

632
00:21:11,422 --> 00:21:14,228
And many nonprofit hospitals are required to

633
00:21:14,228 --> 00:21:16,461
have financial assistance policies.

634
00:21:16,811 --> 00:21:19,325
Try this: always ask for an itemized

635
00:21:19,325 --> 00:21:21,141
bill and check for errors.

636
00:21:21,321 --> 00:21:24,288
Ask the hospital about financial assistance or

637
00:21:24,288 --> 00:21:26,803
charity care, which you may qualify for

638
00:21:26,803 --> 00:21:27,834
even with a job.

639
00:21:28,014 --> 00:21:29,846
And ask for a payment plan with

640
00:21:29,846 --> 00:21:31,914
no interest before the bill goes to

641
00:21:31,914 --> 00:21:32,624
collections.

642
00:21:32,974 --> 00:21:34,425
Cost number nineteen.

643
00:21:34,605 --> 00:21:36,869
Missing out on employer benefits.

644
00:21:37,219 --> 00:21:39,542
When every dollar is needed for this

645
00:21:39,542 --> 00:21:42,641
week, saving for retirement can feel impossible.

646
00:21:42,821 --> 00:21:45,391
So many lower income workers don't contribute

647
00:21:45,391 --> 00:21:48,132
enough to get their employer's retirement match,

648
00:21:48,132 --> 00:21:50,303
which is essentially part of their pay

649
00:21:50,303 --> 00:21:51,331
left on the table.

650
00:21:51,511 --> 00:21:54,398
Some jobs don't offer retirement plans at

651
00:21:54,398 --> 00:21:54,680
all.

652
00:21:55,030 --> 00:21:57,140
There's also the Saver's Credit,

653
00:21:57,140 --> 00:21:59,250
a federal tax credit for low and

654
00:21:59,250 --> 00:22:02,679
moderate income workers who contribute to retirement

655
00:22:02,679 --> 00:22:03,273
accounts.

656
00:22:03,453 --> 00:22:06,169
Many eligible people don't know it exists.

657
00:22:06,349 --> 00:22:09,136
The IRS has information on who qualifies.

658
00:22:09,486 --> 00:22:11,898
Try this: if your employer offers a

659
00:22:11,898 --> 00:22:14,447
match, try to contribute at least one

660
00:22:14,447 --> 00:22:16,997
percent, then increase it as you can.

661
00:22:17,177 --> 00:22:19,301
And check whether you qualify for the

662
00:22:19,301 --> 00:22:21,310
Saver's Credit when you file taxes.

663
00:22:21,661 --> 00:22:22,903
Cost number twenty.

664
00:22:23,083 --> 00:22:24,569
Having no cushion at all.

665
00:22:24,919 --> 00:22:27,210
This is the cost behind almost every

666
00:22:27,210 --> 00:22:28,738
other cost on this list.

667
00:22:28,918 --> 00:22:31,706
Without even a small emergency fund,

668
00:22:31,706 --> 00:22:34,184
every surprise turns into a fee,

669
00:22:34,184 --> 00:22:36,507
a high interest loan or a late

670
00:22:36,507 --> 00:22:37,127
payment.

671
00:22:37,307 --> 00:22:40,280
The cushion is what separates an inconvenience

672
00:22:40,280 --> 00:22:41,184
from a crisis.

673
00:22:41,535 --> 00:22:43,398
Even a few hundred dollars can make

674
00:22:43,398 --> 00:22:44,356
a huge difference.

675
00:22:44,536 --> 00:22:46,485
It can stop an overdraft,

676
00:22:46,485 --> 00:22:48,045
avoid a payday loan,

677
00:22:48,045 --> 00:22:49,760
or cover a car repair.

678
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:51,324
It's not a lot of money,

679
00:22:51,324 --> 00:22:53,168
but it breaks the chain of fees.

680
00:22:53,518 --> 00:22:55,155
Research supports this.

681
00:22:55,335 --> 00:22:58,426
Studies of emergency savings have found that

682
00:22:58,426 --> 00:23:01,237
even small amounts of liquid savings are

683
00:23:01,237 --> 00:23:04,048
linked to fewer missed bill payments and

684
00:23:04,048 --> 00:23:06,086
less use of high cost credit.

685
00:23:06,266 --> 00:23:08,622
A small cushion isn't just comforting.

686
00:23:08,802 --> 00:23:10,381
It changes outcomes.

687
00:23:10,732 --> 00:23:12,664
Try this: start with a goal of

688
00:23:12,664 --> 00:23:14,017
five hundred dollars.

689
00:23:14,197 --> 00:23:16,531
Save small amounts automatically,

690
00:23:16,531 --> 00:23:18,086
and put any windfalls,

691
00:23:18,086 --> 00:23:19,288
like tax refunds,

692
00:23:19,288 --> 00:23:20,490
straight into it.

693
00:23:20,670 --> 00:23:22,448
Keep it in a separate account so

694
00:23:22,448 --> 00:23:24,281
it doesn't get spent by accident.

695
00:23:24,631 --> 00:23:26,233
It's worth saying clearly.

696
00:23:26,413 --> 00:23:28,633
None of the twenty costs we've covered

697
00:23:28,633 --> 00:23:30,035
requires a bad decision.

698
00:23:30,215 --> 00:23:31,344
A low balance,

699
00:23:31,344 --> 00:23:32,230
an old car,

700
00:23:32,230 --> 00:23:35,132
a medical emergency or a gap between

701
00:23:35,132 --> 00:23:37,228
jobs can happen to anyone.

702
00:23:37,408 --> 00:23:39,908
That's why the escape plan focuses on

703
00:23:39,908 --> 00:23:42,138
building systems, a safe account,

704
00:23:42,138 --> 00:23:44,435
a small cushion and better credit,

705
00:23:44,435 --> 00:23:46,057
instead of on willpower.

706
00:23:46,237 --> 00:23:48,875
Systems keep working on the hard weeks,

707
00:23:48,875 --> 00:23:50,498
when willpower runs out.

708
00:23:50,848 --> 00:23:52,928
So how do you escape the poverty

709
00:23:52,928 --> 00:23:53,448
premium?

710
00:23:53,628 --> 00:23:55,741
Here's a step by step plan.

711
00:23:55,921 --> 00:23:57,201
It starts small,

712
00:23:57,201 --> 00:23:58,081
on purpose.

713
00:23:58,431 --> 00:23:59,162
Step one.

714
00:23:59,342 --> 00:24:00,131
Get a safe,

715
00:24:00,131 --> 00:24:01,351
low cost account.

716
00:24:01,531 --> 00:24:03,779
A Bank On certified account or a

717
00:24:03,779 --> 00:24:06,871
credit union account with no overdraft fees.

718
00:24:07,051 --> 00:24:09,758
This alone can save hundreds of dollars

719
00:24:09,758 --> 00:24:11,424
a year in check cashing,

720
00:24:11,424 --> 00:24:13,646
prepaid card and overdraft fees.

721
00:24:13,996 --> 00:24:14,983
Step two.

722
00:24:15,163 --> 00:24:17,504
Build a starter cushion of five hundred

723
00:24:17,504 --> 00:24:17,984
dollars.

724
00:24:18,164 --> 00:24:20,322
Even ten or twenty dollars a week

725
00:24:20,322 --> 00:24:20,846
adds up.

726
00:24:21,026 --> 00:24:22,879
This is the wall that stops the

727
00:24:22,879 --> 00:24:23,835
cascade of fees.

728
00:24:24,015 --> 00:24:24,944
Step three.

729
00:24:25,124 --> 00:24:27,481
Replace the most expensive debt first.

730
00:24:27,661 --> 00:24:29,449
If you have a payday or title

731
00:24:29,449 --> 00:24:31,976
loan, look for a credit union alternative

732
00:24:31,976 --> 00:24:32,839
to pay it off.

733
00:24:33,189 --> 00:24:34,152
Step four.

734
00:24:34,332 --> 00:24:35,784
Start building credit.

735
00:24:35,964 --> 00:24:37,691
A secured credit card,

736
00:24:37,691 --> 00:24:40,360
used for one small bill each month

737
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:41,695
and paid in full,

738
00:24:41,695 --> 00:24:43,893
can build a score over time.

739
00:24:44,073 --> 00:24:46,750
Credit builder loans from credit unions are

740
00:24:46,750 --> 00:24:47,684
another option.

741
00:24:47,864 --> 00:24:50,210
Better credit lowers the cost of almost

742
00:24:50,210 --> 00:24:51,173
everything else.

743
00:24:51,523 --> 00:24:52,510
Step five.

744
00:24:52,690 --> 00:24:53,816
Claim what you're owed.

745
00:24:53,996 --> 00:24:56,924
Check eligibility for the Earned Income Tax

746
00:24:56,924 --> 00:24:58,763
Credit, the Saver's Credit,

747
00:24:58,763 --> 00:25:00,602
health insurance subsidies,

748
00:25:00,602 --> 00:25:02,645
and local assistance programs.

749
00:25:02,825 --> 00:25:05,356
Many eligible people never claim them.

750
00:25:05,536 --> 00:25:07,338
The IRS says about one in five

751
00:25:07,338 --> 00:25:10,100
eligible workers don't claim the Earned Income

752
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:10,761
Tax Credit.

753
00:25:11,111 --> 00:25:12,365
And step six.

754
00:25:12,545 --> 00:25:13,415
Grow income.

755
00:25:13,595 --> 00:25:16,228
A certification, a trade skill,

756
00:25:16,228 --> 00:25:18,011
a better paying role,

757
00:25:18,011 --> 00:25:20,473
or a side income with a clear

758
00:25:20,473 --> 00:25:20,898
goal.

759
00:25:21,078 --> 00:25:22,761
Cutting costs has limits.

760
00:25:22,941 --> 00:25:24,369
Raising income doesn't.

761
00:25:24,549 --> 00:25:26,435
And every dollar of new income,

762
00:25:26,435 --> 00:25:28,200
once the cushion is in place,

763
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:30,329
can go toward the future instead of

764
00:25:30,329 --> 00:25:30,633
fees.

765
00:25:30,983 --> 00:25:32,961
The Earned Income Tax Credit can be

766
00:25:32,961 --> 00:25:35,673
worth thousands of dollars for working families.

767
00:25:35,853 --> 00:25:37,383
But according to the IRS,

768
00:25:37,383 --> 00:25:39,830
about one in five eligible workers don't

769
00:25:39,830 --> 00:25:40,381
claim it.

770
00:25:40,731 --> 00:25:42,780
Let's add up a realistic year for

771
00:25:42,780 --> 00:25:44,395
someone without a cushion,

772
00:25:44,395 --> 00:25:45,700
using modest numbers.

773
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:47,356
Check cashing on paychecks,

774
00:25:47,356 --> 00:25:49,816
perhaps two hundred to three hundred dollars.

775
00:25:49,996 --> 00:25:51,636
A few overdraft fees,

776
00:25:51,636 --> 00:25:53,746
around one hundred dollars.

777
00:25:53,926 --> 00:25:54,934
Three late fees,

778
00:25:54,934 --> 00:25:56,573
about one hundred dollars.

779
00:25:56,753 --> 00:25:59,085
One payday loan rolled over twice,

780
00:25:59,085 --> 00:26:01,966
around one hundred eighty dollars in fees.

781
00:26:02,316 --> 00:26:04,405
Add higher interest on a car loan

782
00:26:04,405 --> 00:26:06,430
because of a lower credit score,

783
00:26:06,430 --> 00:26:09,152
maybe eight hundred to one thousand dollars

784
00:26:09,152 --> 00:26:09,595
a year.

785
00:26:09,775 --> 00:26:11,566
A higher insurance premium,

786
00:26:11,566 --> 00:26:13,026
a few hundred dollars.

787
00:26:13,206 --> 00:26:14,821
Prepaid card fees,

788
00:26:14,821 --> 00:26:16,615
rent to own markups,

789
00:26:16,615 --> 00:26:18,140
and buying small.

790
00:26:18,320 --> 00:26:20,778
Quickly, the total can reach two thousand

791
00:26:20,778 --> 00:26:22,756
to three thousand dollars a year,

792
00:26:22,756 --> 00:26:24,195
depending on the person.

793
00:26:24,545 --> 00:26:26,992
Fees, interest and higher prices can add

794
00:26:26,992 --> 00:26:29,133
up to two to three thousand dollars

795
00:26:29,133 --> 00:26:31,091
a year for a household without a

796
00:26:31,091 --> 00:26:31,580
cushion.

797
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:33,444
That money could have been the cushion.

798
00:26:33,794 --> 00:26:35,303
Here's the cruel irony.

799
00:26:35,483 --> 00:26:37,722
Two to three thousand dollars a year

800
00:26:37,722 --> 00:26:39,588
is more than enough to build a

801
00:26:39,588 --> 00:26:41,392
solid starter emergency fund.

802
00:26:41,572 --> 00:26:43,729
The money that's lost to the poverty

803
00:26:43,729 --> 00:26:46,065
premium is often exactly the money that

804
00:26:46,065 --> 00:26:46,843
could end it.

805
00:26:47,023 --> 00:26:49,021
That's why the first steps of the

806
00:26:49,021 --> 00:26:51,141
escape plan focus on stopping fees,

807
00:26:51,141 --> 00:26:52,654
not on cutting groceries.

808
00:26:53,004 --> 00:26:55,039
If you only do five things from

809
00:26:55,039 --> 00:26:56,417
this video, do these.

810
00:26:56,597 --> 00:26:58,569
They usually save the most money for

811
00:26:58,569 --> 00:26:59,500
the least effort.

812
00:26:59,850 --> 00:27:00,407
Win one.

813
00:27:00,587 --> 00:27:02,312
Move to a no overdraft,

814
00:27:02,312 --> 00:27:03,513
low fee account.

815
00:27:03,693 --> 00:27:04,505
Win two.

816
00:27:04,685 --> 00:27:06,491
Never roll over a payday loan,

817
00:27:06,491 --> 00:27:08,537
and replace it with a credit union

818
00:27:08,537 --> 00:27:09,921
alternative if you can.

819
00:27:10,101 --> 00:27:11,030
Win three.

820
00:27:11,210 --> 00:27:13,533
Call before any bill is late,

821
00:27:13,533 --> 00:27:15,696
and ask for a fee waiver or

822
00:27:15,696 --> 00:27:16,818
a short delay.

823
00:27:17,168 --> 00:27:17,841
Win four.

824
00:27:18,021 --> 00:27:19,035
File your taxes,

825
00:27:19,035 --> 00:27:20,746
even if your income is low,

826
00:27:20,746 --> 00:27:22,964
and check for the Earned Income Tax

827
00:27:22,964 --> 00:27:23,408
Credit.

828
00:27:23,588 --> 00:27:25,693
It can be worth thousands of dollars,

829
00:27:25,693 --> 00:27:27,570
and many eligible people miss it.

830
00:27:27,750 --> 00:27:30,396
Free help is available through the IRS's

831
00:27:30,396 --> 00:27:31,257
VITA program.

832
00:27:31,437 --> 00:27:32,644
And win five.

833
00:27:32,824 --> 00:27:35,306
Automate a tiny weekly savings transfer,

834
00:27:35,306 --> 00:27:36,362
even ten dollars,

835
00:27:36,362 --> 00:27:37,851
into a separate account.

836
00:27:38,031 --> 00:27:39,564
That's the seed of your cushion.

837
00:27:39,914 --> 00:27:42,358
Let's see how much the poverty premium

838
00:27:42,358 --> 00:27:43,580
can cost in a year,

839
00:27:43,580 --> 00:27:46,346
with two neighbors earning the same income.

840
00:27:46,526 --> 00:27:49,282
Tanya cashes her paychecks at a check

841
00:27:49,282 --> 00:27:51,890
cashing store, uses a prepaid card,

842
00:27:51,890 --> 00:27:53,306
and has no savings.

843
00:27:53,486 --> 00:27:55,087
When her car broke down,

844
00:27:55,087 --> 00:27:57,289
she took a payday loan and rolled

845
00:27:57,289 --> 00:27:58,223
it over twice.

846
00:27:58,403 --> 00:28:00,965
She paid several overdraft and late fees

847
00:28:00,965 --> 00:28:01,990
during the year.

848
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:03,002
Add it up,

849
00:28:03,002 --> 00:28:05,587
and Tanya paid easily over one thousand

850
00:28:05,587 --> 00:28:08,172
dollars in fees and interest that year,

851
00:28:08,172 --> 00:28:10,292
possibly closer to two thousand.

852
00:28:10,472 --> 00:28:12,202
Not because she was careless.

853
00:28:12,382 --> 00:28:14,757
Because every surprise cost her extra,

854
00:28:14,757 --> 00:28:16,757
and she had no other options she

855
00:28:16,757 --> 00:28:17,444
knew about.

856
00:28:17,795 --> 00:28:19,908
Her neighbor Maria earns the same.

